Thanks Hans and Ben! The (H + t(H))/2 method works perfectly. Much appreciated!

Peter

On Thursday, June 16, 2022 at 11:09:30 PM UTC-6 Hans Skaug wrote:
> AD is only accurate to numerical precision. This applies also to the Hessian.
> Ben's suggestion of using (H + t(H))/2 seems to be the best way of handling the problem at hand.
>
> On Friday, June 17, 2022 at 2:58:05 AM UTC+2 Ben Bolker wrote:
> > Also fairly ignorant, but:
> >
> > * it's kind of surprising that the AD-generated Hessian is asymmetric. I
> > can imagine the computations of (i,j) and (j,i) elements being
> > different, but don't quite understand why AD would bother computing
> > both? You might try symmetrizing by computing (H + t(H))/2, that would
> > be cheap and might help a little bit?
> >
> > My limited and out-of-date knowledge of numerical optimization
> > methods is that people had decided that in general the Hessian-based
> > algorithms weren't in general sufficiently much better than conjugate
> > gradients or quasi-Newton methods that it was worth the extra cost of
> > computing the Hessian. I don't know if having AD-based rather than
> > symbolic Hessians changes that calculus or not ...
> >
> > If it were me I would try them both out on some test cases and see
> > how it went ...
> >
> > On 2022-06-16 4:13 p.m., pthomp...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > Hi all, I've been using TMB for about a year for some reasonably complex
> > > ecological modelling problems and it has generally worked very well. The
> > > model I'm currently working on seems to be having some optimization
> > > issues, and frankly I'm not sure if these issues are a result of the R
> > > /optimx /package I'm using for optimization, or TMB itself. Some of the
> > > optimization methods within /optimx /are second-order methods that
> > > require the computation (or approximation) of a Hessian. I figured an
> > > exact, automatically differentiated Hessian from TMB would be best, so I
> > > included /likelihood$he/ as an argument in my /optimx /call along with
> > > /likelihood$fn/ and /likelihood$gr/.
> > >
> > > When I ran this, R returned an error indicating that my Hessian "might
> > > be wrong". I looked into this further and apparently optimx runs an
> > > internal function, called /optimx.check/, that compares the
> > > user-supplied Hessian function to a numerically derived Hessian
> > > (generated by a function in the /numDeriv/ R package). I manually
> > > compared the two Hessians and indeed, they are a bit different. I would
> > > naturally assume that in truth, the AD-computed Hessian would be more
> > > accurate than the numerical Hessian, but I don't have enough experience
> > > to know for sure. For what it's worth, in my case, the numerically
> > > derived Hessian is symmetric, whereas TMB's Hessian is slightly
> > > asymmetric (largest differences on the order of 10^-11).
> > >
> > > I know this may be a bit out of the scope of this group, but any
> > > thoughts on how to proceed? Maybe it's best to just use the numerically
> > > calculated Hessian, but the likelihood function is very noisy so I was
> > > thinking an AD-generated Hessian could help. I'm happy to share more
> > > information if necessary.
> > >
> > > Thank you!
> > > Peter
